<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Idar State</h1><p class="st-meta">1728 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Himmatnagar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Rathore Rajput state in the hills of north-eastern Gujarat, ruled by a branch of the Jodhpur royal house.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Idar had an older line of Rao chiefs, but in 1728 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anand-singh-of-idar">Anand Singh</a> and Rai Singh, brothers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abhai-singh-of-marwar">Abhai Singh of Jodhpur</a>, took the fortress with Mughal sanction and founded a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> pressure soon forced it to pay tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaekwads">Gaekwads</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/baroda-state">Baroda State</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Idar was the leading state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahi-kantha-agency">Mahi Kantha Agency</a>, governed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> over hilly, partly tribal country. Its most famous ruler was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-of-idar">Sir Pratap Singh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>, the soldier and friend of the British royal family, who held Idar from 1902 to 1911. The town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himmatnagar">Ahmednagar</a>, which became the capital, was later renamed Himmatnagar after Maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himmat-singh-of-idar">Himmat Singh</a> (ruled 1931–49).</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Idar acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and in 1948 was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a>; since 1960 its lands, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himmatnagar">Himmatnagar</a>, are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabarkantha-district">Sabarkantha district</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/idar-state.jpg" alt="Idar State" width="207" height="300"><figcaption>Image: Rudolf Swoboda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Pratap_Singh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1728 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Himmatnagar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Anand Singh, Pratap Singh, Himmat Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Idar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=idar-state" aria-label="Idar State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6625 1299 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Idar State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6945 1555L7364 1601M6945 1555L7364 1602M6945 1555L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6945 1555h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6945 1555h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=idar-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
